ponges at you  get out paper whatever they can't  because the scene is already set and  you're just the live instrument that  goes into it to make the entire scene  come alive again I think it's just  beautiful because it takes us back to  being kids you know what I mean back to  square one  all right we have some actual questions  from from the audience and I have one  question of my own that I want to ask  too so the first one is what as actors  can we do to prepare for the technology  in casting for the future projects and  new skills or anything like that I think  that's a great question just briefly I  think do not think of technology because  this is the one thing that they cannot  quite grasp yet is you come in with your  talent your bag of emotions and what it  is you want to portray about a character  and just show them what you have to  offer absolutely um it does as somebody  said earlier I think it's been said a  couple of times to look maybe and II set  it to what it opens up in terms of like  the end of typecasting in a way in that  way though if there's other ways that  one doesn't need to think in that in  terms of typecasting but if you can  embrace it or bring in your performance  and explore as far as you can whether  it's an orangutan or a nine-foot tall  whoever if you can go there in your  thinking and your imagination and in  your own exploration of that then  there's possibilities of these ways to  capture it so I don't think it's about  the technology at all I think it's  actually about just even going further  deeper in as an actor in setting  yourself free to that I also say that  where all this stuff is going is trying  to strip away a lot of the the  technology you know despite that a lot  of the tech that you're seeing and that  the current state of the art you know  where it's rapidly going is trying to  strip all that away to make this as  natural and friendly for the performers  as possible and we do really feel  strongly that everything that we're  doing we're trying to create the best  conditions for performance  we can and we strongly believe that you  know two characters are in a scene they  got to be playing their performances  against each other and not you know this  person's captured three months later but  you know that when they say I need a  younger version of CCH pounder it's  gonna be so much easier now but I mean  as well the the problems in the  preparation that's kind of our job to  figure out like I don't think from the  actors it's not their problem that's  that's the visual effects problem like  how do we remove all the limitations and  make it as easy as possible okay the  next question is can you elaborate more  on the animator artistry that goes into  helping and bring these performances to  life and it bears brings to mind  something I was always taught when I  start I think when I was working on t2  at ILM and somebody just came up to me  said animation is acting and and every  ball of everybody had to do a scene that  really had to animate you know the t1000  there I had a mirror and everybody  looked into the mirror and Matt was  saying they still do it we still did I  of course yeah I mean we're all actors  that don't get behind the camera we're  behind the camera in the form of you  know giant creatures and things like  that but what all the same principles we  study and things that we look at we look  at other performances and when we're  breaking down a character that's the  first thing we do no matter what it is  but if it's an animal we have tons of  references if we're out there also  shooting reference of each other we have  an animators acting room in the building  for us to go into and do whatever roll  around and you know a lot of the stuff  we have to do is pretty physical so you  know we've got certain animators that  we've actually typed casted for  different things so it is very much  performance driven and how much of that  we do like I said before varies from  project to project so sometimes it's a  hundred percent keyframe we take it over  and it's it's the animators performance  and it may be just an actor's voice  other times like I said it's very we  want to stay as true to whatever you  know needed to be done for that so you  know if it's something like more easier  you know you want to stay pretty darn  close to what that performance is so  it's less invention and more about you  know getting that performance across  that you want but yeah it's  we'll do everything in the book but the  one thing that has changed a little bit  over the years as animators kind of  trained and you know I from kind of a  disney style but that doesn't work in  this case so you kind of have to throw  the rulebook out a little bit and really  study the way things move and this is  where motion capture is fantastic for  animators because there's a lot of weird  stuff if you animated that you'd get it  rejected but you know when it's motion  capture and they do a weird foot thing  it's it just brings that extra layer of  life to it so I'm fine I think it's a  fantastic tool for bringing performance  okay another question is do you need  formal training if to do most  performance captioners so where do you  study  motion capture websites whatever right I  mean I think woody Schultz might be in  the audience at this point but but one  of the things is it seems that each  company because the genre of their film  is so special and secretive my  experience is that they teach you  everything on-site and that's probably  why it takes so long but once you've  learned something then you're actually  quite equipped whatever the next film is  coming and from that from the visual  effects side I would say work with as  many talented companies as you can work  with I was fortunate enough to work at  John's company for a while in touch with  magic I learned a great deal right and  and because you if you try to work with  talented people you'll learn how they're  addressing the current technology and  all you know all the big talent  companies NPC DNA ILM what are all just  pushing the boundaries as hard as they  can right every day Digital Domain as  well right and in terms of preparing to  do this kind of work the advice I'd give  to actors is is do your best to just  ignore it you know they don't do  anything different act as you would if  none of this technology was present  because that's what we're really  interested in and if you do try to learn  the technology and your last thing about  it is going to be different next year  anyway and then one other things here  too the sake the technology also most  the technology is really geared around  how to  help the actors in their performances  and one of the things we've been able to  do and see a lot more of is getting the  actors in for initial sessions then be  able to turn around a version of them as  their creature or their character so as  they're actually doing the performance  unset as they're doing there's like  performances they can actually see what  the character's going to look like how  much of that performance comes through  so it's no longer quite as blind as it  had been in the past  yeah good example for me recently and  that was on rogue one we had alan tudyk  playing a droid character and we invited  him up to two ILM and he spent an  afternoon in our motion capture volume  with thing we call the magic mirror  where he could see his his performance  in real-time mapped on to the droid  character so that he had a chance to  play around to see what sort of motions  looked good on the droid and how loose  does he want to be does he want to  extract some more stiff and robotic or  you want to kind of have a service  slouchy attitude and he got to spend an  afternoon trying stuff and seeing what  felt right for the character and he he  was able to internalize that and then  bring that to the set so he sort of went  into that you know with some confidence  that what he was doing was gonna look  good on the character and I remembered  my thing about about the the truth it  seems as if finding the truth in the  performances is preserving what's true  for actors in the director and then the  animators when they need to directed by  the by the director they come in and  they they try to apply the same truth so  it's it pulls true and doesn't break  character right like whatever we do we  definitely don't want it to be any  different than the character you guys  are playing because that would just blow  it horribly yeah I think the truth is is  that we probably have to wrap this up  truth is right one last one last  question is do you think performance  capture will ever be nominated in the  acting acting category gosh I hope so  so do I  well so many people do not know what it  is and didn't recognize it the first 20  times it came around well this concludes  our show tonight and remember the future  can be scary but also exciting one thing  for sure is that will always happen in  the change will always happen in the  world of entertainment but telling a  good story will never go on a style  please check out the performance capture  suit in the lobby on your way out and if  you have time to fill out a survey to  suggest what other topics you'd like to  see in these presentations please do so  I just like to thank all you guys for  doing this thank you so much well thanks  for coming thank you all for coming  tonight thank you good night  [Applause]
 

